x86_64-linux  The same two differences handler-exit-end-catch.nonames sets out, and neither is a name spelled differently. clang emits `__cxa_free_exception`, the cleanup it lays down for a throw whose *object's own constructor* throws, and `__cxa_get_exception_ptr`, which the Itanium ABI asks a handler to call before `__cxa_begin_catch` when it copy-initialises a by-value parameter - `byValue()` here is what reaches the second. cxx1 emits neither: it copies through the pointer `__cxa_begin_catch` returns, which is the same pointer, and has no cleanup around the construction of a thrown object. The one this case is about, `__cxa_end_catch`, agrees - both compilers call it on the unwinder's path out of a handler, which is what the ledger line measures. The cxx1-only `_ZN1AC1Ei`, `_ZN1AD1Ev`, `_ZN1EC1Ei`, `_ZN1EC1ERKS_` and `_ZN1ED1Ev` are the older trap: clang emits only the C2 and D2 forms of a constructor and destructor defined inside their class on this target.
arm64-darwin  Only the clang-only pair, `___cxa_free_exception` and `___cxa_get_exception_ptr`, for the reasons above. This target emits both constructor forms whatever the definition's place, so the C1/D1 half does not arise, and every other name agrees.
